  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively provide several relevant ingredients but not the later result. R12 establishes the exact AlCoCrFeNi2.1 alloy and its eutectic FCC/B2 lamellar structure. R5 and R17 show that chemically ordered L12/B2-type states can arise in closely related Al-containing CoCrFeNi alloys, while R6 links local ordering to dislocation resistance. R2 and R18 connect thermomechanically generated coherent structures with planar slip, localized shear, and stress concentration, and R13 supplies related elastic-load-transfer concepts. However, no record describes intermediate-temperature aging of AlCoCrFeNi2.1, the maximum in L12 ordering and jagged phase-boundary density near 600 °C, or their reversal near 750 °C. Thus the literature points toward thermally controlled ordering and interface-mediated deformation, but proposing the specific aging treatment and non-monotonic ductile-brittle-ductile mechanism would still require a substantial new idea."
